TI  - Underwater sounds near a fuel receiving facility in western Hong Kong:: relevance to dolphins
T2  - MARINE ENVIRONMENTAL RESEARCH
LA  - English
KW  - underwater sounds
KW  - noise
KW  - tankers
KW  - mammals
KW  - marine mammals
KW  - ecosystem disturbance
KW  - disturbance
KW  - environmental impact
KW  - Indo-Pacific humpbacked dolphin Sousa chinensis
KW  - finless porpoise, Neophocaena phocaenoides
AB  - Western Hong Kong is home to two species of marine mammals: Indo-Pacific humpbacked dolphins (Sousa chincnsis) and finless porpoises (Neophocaena phocaenoides). Both are threatened in many parts of their range in southeast Asia [for example, International Biological Research Institute Reports 9 (1997). 41, Asian Marine Biology 14 (1997) 111]. In 1998, when the new Hong Kong International Airport opened in western Hong Kong. small tankers (about 100 m long, cargo capacity about 6300 metric tons) began delivering fuel to the Aviation Fuel Receiving Facility (AFRF)just off Sha Chau Island. north of the airport. Calibrated sound recordings were taken over a 4-day period from a quiet, anchored boat at distances 80 2000 m from aviation fuel delivery activities at the AFRF. From the recordings, 143 sections were selected for analysis, Narrowband spectral densities on the sound pressures were computed, and one-third octave band levels were derived for center frequencies from 10 to 16,000 Hz. Broadband levels, viz. 10-20,000 Hz. were also computed. The results showed that the Sha Chan area is normally noisy underwater, with the lowest broadband levels measured corresponding to those expected during a storm at sea (sea state 6). This background noise is believed to come largely from heavy vessel traffic in the Urmston Road to the north and east of Sha Chau and front vessels in the Pearl River Estuary to the West, The sound levels from the AFRF tankers are comparable to the levels measured from similar- and smaller-sized supply vessels supporting offshore oil exploration. The strongest sounds recorded were from a tanker leaving the AFRF at distance 100 m from the hydrophone, for which the one-third octave band level at 100 Hz was 141 dB re 1 muPa (spectrum level 127 dB re 1 muPa(2)/Hz) and the 10-20,000 Hz broadband level was 146 dB. At distances of 100 m or more and frequencies above 300 Hz, the one-third octave band levels were less than 130 dB (spectrum level 111 dB re 1 muPa(2)/Hz) and decreased with increasing frequency and distance. At distances greater than about 500 m. AFRF-associated sounds were negligible, masked by the generally high noise level of the area and attenuated by poor transmission in the very shallow water ( < 10 m). Because it is believed that humpbacked dolphins and finless porpoises are not very sensitive to sounds below 300 Hz, the Airport Authority Hong Kong (AA) Stil) stipulated that dedicated terminal vessels not radiate underwater sounds at spectrum levels greater than 110 dB re 1 muPa(2)/Hz at frequencies above 300 Hz and distances greater than 300 m. The spectrum levels at 300 Hz and higher frequencies of sounds from the tankers arriving. departing, or off-loading at AFRF were less than 110 dB re 1 muPa(2)/Hz even at distariccs of 200 in or less. The AA stipulation was met. However, it is presently unknown whether the generally strong noise levels of western Hong Kong inhibit acoustically based feeding and communication. or result in increased stress or permanent shifts in hearing, thresholds. (C) 2002 Elsevier Science Ltd. All rights reserved.
