TY  - JOUR
AU  - LI, YM
TI  - THE TONGUE OF THE BAIJI, LIPOTES-VEXILLIFER
T2  - ACTA ZOOLOGICA SINICA
LA  - Chinese
AB  - Seven specimens of the tongue of L. vexillifer were studied. There are 2 pits on the dorsum of the tongue, which is divided into a front and a rear part by a connecting line between the 2 pits. The former consists of the apex linguae and corpus linguae; and the latter, radix linguae. These 2 pits correspond to the pits of the V-shaped row of the marine dolphins. There are 2 or 3 warty projections protruding from the bottom of the pit. Taste buds are found in the epithelium of the warty projections and the lateral wall of the pit. The taste buds in the young adult are fewer in number than those in the calf; no taste buds were found in old animals. There are many papillary projections along the anterolateral margins of the tongue. They are numerous in the calf, but much less in the adult. In the old animal, the projections became atrophied. Lingual glands, sero-mucous in nature, are well developed in adults. They open on the dorsal surface from the middle region of the posterior part of the tongue to the vicinity of the epiglottis and on the wall of the oropharynx. No sulcus terminalis and foramen caecum as well as the lingual papillae (filiform, fungiform, foliate and circumvallate papillae) can be found. For comparison, tongues of Inia boliviensis, marine dolphins [Sousa chinensis, Delphinus delphis, Tursiops truncatus, Stenella frontalis, Pseudorca crassidens, Neophocaena asiaeorientalis] and terrestrial animals were observed.
